#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# first

#% text
# `first(xs)` gives the first element of the list `xs`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `first(xs)` is `xs[1]`. It works on any compound term: `first(f(a, b))` is `a`.

#% item
# The first element of an empty list is a failure; `first(xs, d)` gives `d` instead (`d` is evaluated like every argument).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
first([a, b, c])
#% output
#= a

#% cell
first([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= [1, 2]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A default for an empty list:

#% cell
first([], none)
#% output
#= none

#% text
# The first argument of a term:

#% cell
first(f(x, y))
#% output
#= x

#% text
# The first solution of an equation:

#% cell
first(solve(x^2 - 5x + 6 == 0, x))
#% output
#= [x -> 2]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The smallest prime factor, from the factorisation:

#% cell
first(first(factor_integer(1001)))
#% output
#= 7

#% text
# One solution put into an expression, as a script picks a branch:

#% cell
t /. first(solve(t^2 == 9, t))
#% output
#= -3

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `first` is the same as indexing with 1, `last` with -1:

#% cell
xs = [5, 6, 7]
[first(xs), xs[1], last(xs), xs[-1]]
#% output
#= [5, 5, 7, 7]

#% text
# `rest` is everything after the first element:

#% cell
prepend(rest(xs), first(xs)) === xs
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# An empty list has no first element:

#% cell
first([])
#% output
#! failure(index, 1, 0)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `last`, `rest`, `most`, `take`, `part`
